  "id": "jingju_yunbai",
  "name": "Jingju yunbai stage-speech",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "In jingju (Beijing/Peking opera), which crystallized in the Qing capital after the 1790 arrival of the Anhui troupes, stage-speech falls into two registers: yunbai, a heightened stylized declamation using an archaic rhyme-speech pronunciation, and jingbai, closer to colloquial Beijing dialect used chiefly by clown (chou) and certain female roles. Both are delivered with bright, forward, tonally exaggerated projection, are role-type specific, and are set against the jinghu fiddle and percussion, as codified as the sung arias themselves. Actors train by role-type in academies descending from masters such as Mei Lanfang (1894-1961). It is documented in the China National Peking Opera Company records and extensive 20th-century recordings.",
